{
 "ep": 7,
 "total": 140,
 "title": "PostgreSQL Data Directory: What Lives Inside PGDATA?",
 "phase": "Foundations",
 "lines": [
  {
   "say": "What is actually stored inside PGDATA?",
   "cap": "What is actually stored inside *PGDATA?*"
  },
  {
   "say": "This is episode seven of the PostgreSQL DBA series. Today: a tour of the data directory.",
   "cap": "Episode 7: a tour of the *data* *directory.*"
  },
  {
   "say": "PGDATA is a cluster's data directory. initdb creates it, and by default every database's files live there.",
   "cap": "*initdb* creates it. Database files live *under* *it.*",
   "cue": "initdb"
  },
  {
   "say": "The name comes from the PGDATA environment variable, an alternative to initdb's dash D option.",
   "cap": "Named after the *PGDATA* variable, an alternative to *-D.*",
   "cue": "variable"
  },
  {
   "say": "Where is it? On this Ubuntu test server, SHOW data_directory points into slash var slash lib.",
   "cap": "*SHOW data_directory* tells you where it is."
  },
  {
   "say": "But config_file points into slash etc. Ubuntu keeps its config files outside PGDATA.",
   "cap": "Config files *can* *live* *elsewhere.* Ubuntu uses */etc.*",
   "cue": "config_file"
  },
  {
   "say": "Here is a fresh cluster made with initdb. The output is trimmed.",
   "cap": "A fresh cluster made with *initdb.* Output trimmed."
  },
  {
   "say": "base: one subdirectory per database, named by its OID.",
   "cap": "*base:* one folder per database, named by *OID.*"
  },
  {
   "say": "global: cluster-wide tables, like pg_database.",
   "cap": "*global:* cluster-wide tables like *pg_database.*"
  },
  {
   "say": "pg_wal: the write-ahead log files.",
   "cap": "*pg_wal:* the write-ahead log files."
  },
  {
   "say": "pg_tblspc: symbolic links to tablespaces.",
   "cap": "*pg_tblspc:* symbolic links to *tablespaces.*"
  },
  {
   "say": "pg X-act: transaction commit status data.",
   "cap": "*pg_xact:* transaction *commit* *status.*"
  },
  {
   "say": "PG_VERSION holds the major version. postmaster.pid is a lock file, and it's not there after the server shuts down.",
   "cap": "*PG_VERSION* and the *postmaster.pid* lock file.",
   "cue": "postmaster.pid"
  },
  {
   "say": "Config files usually sit here too, and postgresql.auto.conf stores ALTER SYSTEM settings.",
   "cap": "Config files, plus *postgresql.auto.conf* for *ALTER SYSTEM.*"
  },
  {
   "say": "Now a real table. In appdb, the database OID is one six three eight four.",
   "cap": "*appdb* has OID *16384.*"
  },
  {
   "say": "pg_relation_filepath of orders returns base, slash, one six three eight four, slash, one six three nine two.",
   "cap": "*pg_relation_filepath* shows the table's file.",
   "cue": "returns"
  },
  {
   "say": "So one table has three files: the main data, a free space map, and a visibility map.",
   "cap": "Main file, *free* *space* *map,* *visibility* *map.*",
   "cue": "three"
  },
  {
   "say": "Past one gigabyte, a table or index is split into one gigabyte segments: dot one, dot two, and so on.",
   "cap": "Over *1* *GB:* split into *segments* .1, .2 ...",
   "cue": "segments"
  },
  {
   "say": "Careful: files are named by filenode, which is not always the table's OID. TRUNCATE and REINDEX can change it.",
   "cap": "File name = *filenode,* not always the *OID.*"
  },
  {
   "say": "Big mistake: deleting files in PGDATA by hand, even when the disk is full.",
   "cap": "*Never* delete files in PGDATA by hand."
  },
  {
   "say": "PostgreSQL removes or recycles old WAL itself. If pg_wal keeps growing, check archive_command and replication slots.",
   "cap": "pg_wal growing? Check *archiving* and *replication* *slots.*",
   "cue": "growing"
  },
  {
   "say": "Security: initdb gives access only to the PostgreSQL user, and optionally its group. Owner-only mode is seven zero zero.",
   "cap": "Owner-only access: mode *0700.*"
  },
  {
   "say": "Recap: base holds databases, global holds cluster-wide tables, and pg_wal holds the WAL.",
   "cap": "Recap: *base,* *global* and *pg_wal.*"
  },
  {
   "say": "pg_relation_filepath finds any table's file. And never delete files by hand.",
   "cap": "*pg_relation_filepath* finds files. *Never* delete by hand."
  },
  {
   "say": "Next up: tables, rows, pages and tuples. Follow so you don't miss it, and send this to a friend who's learning PostgreSQL.",
   "cap": "Next up: *pages* and *tuples.* *Follow* and share."
  }
 ],
